DosSantos_etal_2020-E004,DosSantos_etal_2020_Table1,Br_I/mg,"Table 1, p. 4625, row ""Dendrohyrax dorsalis Br""",10 129,10129,publication_error,2123,"Printed 10 129, above every one of the species' own parts (Cx 1832, Cb 1235, RoB 3299), which a whole-brain density cannot be. I / (mass x 1000) = 27,176,885 / 12802 = 2122.9, and the mass-weighted mean of the three printed part densities is 2,124; that mass-weighted identity holds to a median 0.03% across the table's 94 testable cases. The same row's N/mg (39 412) is consistent with N / mass. Agrees with the private check against the authors' own data (restricted repo), which is not quoted here.",confirmed,,AI assistant (DosSantos_etal_2020_Table1_internal_check.R) 2026-11-21,2026-11-21,2026-11-21,Printed value read on the PDF page. Substituted in the cell-counts merge (guarded step 3.4e).
DosSantos_etal_2020-E005,DosSantos_etal_2020_Table1,Hp_I/N,"Table 1, p. 4625, row ""Tragelaphus strepsiceros Hp""",3.946,3.946,publication_error,1.034,"The Table 1 footnote (p. 4627) defines I as (I/N) x N, so I/N = I / N must hold; 150 of the table's 153 rows satisfy it within rounding. Here I / N = 29,326,563 / 28,363,281 = 1.034 against a printed 3.946 (+282%). I is vouched for by Cx I = Ctx I + Hp I (within 1.0%) and N by N/mg = N / mass (within 0.02%), which leaves the printed ratio as the wrong cell. Agrees with the private check against the authors' own data (restricted repo), which is not quoted here.",confirmed,,AI assistant (DosSantos_etal_2020_Table1_internal_check.R) 2026-11-21,2026-11-21,2026-11-21,Printed value read on the PDF page. Substituted in the cell-counts merge (guarded step 3.4e).
DosSantos_etal_2020-E006,DosSantos_etal_2020_Table1,Hp_I/mg,"Table 1, p. 4625, row ""Tragelaphus strepsiceros Hp""",4710,4710,publication_error,2682,"Printed 4710 against I / (mass x 1000) = 29,326,563 / 10936 = 2,682 (+76%). Independent of the I column: densities are mass-additive, and the printed Cx (3718 /mg, 213.37 g) and Ctx (3772 /mg, 202.434 g) leave 2,718 /mg for the 10.936 g hippocampus, not 4710; with 4710 the Cx density would be 3,820, a 2.7% miss where the table's median miss is 0.03%. The same row's I/N is also wrong (E005). Agrees with the private check against the authors' own data (restricted repo), which is not quoted here.",confirmed,,AI assistant (DosSantos_etal_2020_Table1_internal_check.R) 2026-11-21,2026-11-21,2026-11-21,Printed value read on the PDF page. Substituted in the cell-counts merge (guarded step 3.4e).
DosSantos_etal_2020-E007,DosSantos_etal_2020_Table1,Cx_C,"Table 1, p. 4627, row ""Cebus apella Cx""",3035 947 220,3035947220,publication_error,3720000000,"Table 1 gives 100 I/C = 3.49% and 100 I/(C-N) = 5.14% for this row; Figure 5 (p. 4638) plots 2.85% (panel D) and 3.85% (panel H) for the same species. The eight primate cortex markers were read off the figure (internal_checks/DosSantos_etal_2020_Figure5_primate_cortex_points.py) and named by neuronal-density order; the five self-consistent primate rows reproduce Table 1 to within 0.9% (D) and 2.3% (H), so the reading is calibrated on the paper itself. N, I, I/N, I/mg, N/mg and mass of this row are mutually consistent, which leaves C: I / (D reading) = 3,719,616,374 and N + I / (H reading) = 3,722,000,364, two independent panels agreeing to 0.1%. Also: Figure 5A's Primata histogram has three species in the 2-3% bin (two without this one), and the Primata Cx mean of 100 I/C recomputed from Table 1 is 3.95% where Table 5 prints 3.75%. Proposed value = mean of the two panel readings, to three significant figures (reading uncertainty about 1%). Agrees with the private check against the authors' own data (restricted repo), which is not quoted here.",confirmed,,AI assistant (DosSantos_etal_2020_Table1_internal_check.R) 2026-11-21,2026-11-21,2026-11-21,"Printed value read on the PDF page. The proposed value is derived from the published figure, not printed anywhere. Substituted in the cell-counts merge (guarded step 3.4e), where it only affects the within-source microglia-per-cell ratio; merged total cells come from the primary source."
